outcome.technical:
Design T1. tools/stream-cost.py prints priced spans:
- planning, before the cycle, the task cycle, sign-off, fix task, close and unattributed, with the bounds read off parsed shell words and the Skill input;
- the task cycle on its own;
- injected command bodies sized as their own source;
- reference reads by span;
- one line per dispatch with its start read and written;
- the longest gap per context.

A background-launched dispatch gets no rebuilt final. tools/measure-context.py gains --sections, using the design's classification table, plus entries for /audit:phase add, /audit:task add, and the run form before and at sign-off.

Targets met on feature-C-1, whole-C-2, whole-C-3, whole-C-1 and whole-C-2-r:
- class sums 77811/27767/25037/23089;
- cycle 6-13 with 8 requests, 21857 tokens, task 0.704895, file 0.212897, output 11374;
- whole-C-2 planning 1 and no cycle;
- whole-C-3 planning 1-12 and cycle 15-36 holding P1.1-P1.3;
- command bodies of 15808 and 16035 tokens;
- the unattributed row gone on C-3 and C-1 with totals unchanged;
- whole-C-2-r at -5043, named as a hidden mid-agent request, with a rule proposed and not implemented;
- background and foreground twins priced equal.

Cycle and fix-task costs move at the fourth decimal because the phantom finals no longer absorb Sonnet's residual write. Command bodies are classed run.

Selftests: 40/40 and 18/18; ruff, vermin and the prose lint are clean. 23 scratch-clone mutations each turned their case red. The red-first helper refused, because the task declares no test file.

Design T4. New entry point governance/drive-phase.py with `next <phase>`. It runs every step that needs no judgement as the existing verb in a subprocess: audit-lock, audit-task start/block/finding/done --from-return, audit-lookup brief, run-test-gate --record, stamp-verification compare and commit-task-work. It prints exactly one instruction:
- dispatch, with the brief path;
- decide, from DECISIONS, answered through --answer;
- done.

A refused verb stops the drive and relays the verb's own words. The recorded gate and the stamp grading run before the reviewer's brief. A stale stamp names the fields that moved. Drive state lives in stateDir/drive/<phase>.json.

Seams are left for the next tasks: reviewer_due (T6), STEPS as the single source of printed lines (T2), subcommands for submit (T8), and finish() for sign-off (T10).

tools/stream-cost.py reads a driver session's task cycle from the driver's own started/closed words (sp12, with the over-fire twin sp13).

The fixture micro-test drives a three-task wave with real verbs and filed returns:
- 12 model-facing requests for three tasks, plus the opening next;
- every print at or under 300 bytes;
- the gate recorded before each reviewer dispatch;
- a stale stamp naming the moved fields;
- a refused verb stopping in its own words.

Each property is shown red by an in-suite mutant (dp2m, dp3m, dp4m, dp5m, dr2m), and red-first is proved at HEAD. The new file owes all five CLAUDE.md items, and they are present.

Reported, not fixed: every task's stamp comes out stale on dirtyDigest, because filing the return and recording the gate dirty the evidence directory. That is recorded as its own task.

Design T8.

drive-phase.py submit <task|phase> --role executor|reviewer [--head SHA] [-- <test command>] reads the return on stdin and works in this order:
1. it checks the shape;
2. it refuses an already-filed return before anything runs;
3. it runs stamp-verification red when a test command is given, and requires one on a tdd task;
4. it takes the stamp last;
5. it files through the write-once file-return. The phase return passes --head through the same path.

Exit codes: 0 when filed, 1 when refused with nothing written, 2 on a usage error.

Cases:
- the missing-stamp pair ds1/ds2;
- the second-filing refusal ds3;
- the malformed-return pair ds4/ds5;
- the tdd cases ds6 and ds7;
- the phase return ds8;
- the relative-manifest submit ds9;
- the mutant twins ds1m, ds3m and ds4m.

Every drive in the suite now plays agents through submit.

Both agent prompts lose the procedure prose submit replaces, measured by tools/measure-context.py: executor 19591 to 6762 bytes, reviewer 20937 to 6986, against ceilings of 9000 and 7000. AGENT_CEILINGS with ceiling_breaches holds them in the sweep (mc19, with the red twin mc20). Every pinned sentence and the README's tools rows are kept. omitClaudeMd, preloaded skills and the one-hour cacheTtl are not taken, for the reasons C13 gives, recorded beside the ceiling.

The executor, reviewer and phase briefs print one resolved submit line, with a tdd task's test command after --, and no separate stamp, red or file-return step. bf14 was proved red at HEAD.

The README, the build guide and prove-gates follow. execute-task.md and task.md still describe the agent filing directly; that is P118.9's rewrite. The reviewer prompt's margin to its ceiling is small by design.

_tree_stamp.dirty_digest(before, excluded) drops a porcelain line only when every path on it, a rename's two paths included, is under excluded. It uses _outside, the same segment-boundary predicate content uses, so there is no second list. tested_state passes dirty_excluded only from take; compare goes through take, so both sides agree. The excluded set is _evidence_io.recorded_paths, which already covers the evidence directory and filed returns under evidence/returns, the journal, the plan index and the shards. _evidence_io.py and stamp-verification.py needed no change.

run-test-gate's recorded dirtyDigest is deliberately unchanged, so rows already recorded still grade to the same bytes (tsd6). A stamp taken by an older version while recorder paths were dirty reads stale once, which is the safe direction.

Cases tsd1-tsd6 and sv18-sv21 cover these:
- a filed return alone stays fresh;
- a recorded gate alone stays fresh;
- a source edit still reads stale;
- a lookalike of the evidence path outside the directory still reads stale.

They were red at HEAD, with over-fire and segment mutations shown red in a scratch copy. On the driver fixture the per-task stamp now reads current, except for the task whose file really moved.
